WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.120
In Chapter One, the GPU doubles five input values.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.180
Let’s follow one of them: one point zero becomes two point zero.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:15.504
The GPU program supplies instructions: individual operations such as a load, a multiply, or a store.

00:00:15.724 --> 00:00:21.420
A streaming multiprocessor, or S M, provides the hardware that performs those instructions.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.971
The input and output arrays are in GPU memory, outside the S M.

00:00:27.991 --> 00:00:31.809
Thread Zero’s load requests the value at input index zero.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:35.101
Load and store units handle memory instructions.

00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:42.148
A load brings a value from memory into a register: a small, fast storage location inside the S M.

00:00:42.368 --> 00:00:46.592
Registers hold the working values that arithmetic instructions use.

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:52.999
The request can take time; this diagram jumps ahead to when one point zero arrives in a register.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.099
The input array still holds its original value.

00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:58.815
Nothing has been written to the output array yet.

00:01:00.835 --> 00:01:05.379
The multiply instruction uses the loaded value and the multiplier two.

00:01:05.599 --> 00:01:10.356
Floating point arithmetic units perform calculations on floating point values.

00:01:10.576 --> 00:01:14.715
Here, they produce two point zero and return the result to a register.

00:01:14.935 --> 00:01:19.393
Our values use thirty two bit floating point, often called F P thirty two.

00:01:19.613 --> 00:01:24.840
These arithmetic units are shared by threads, rather than permanently owned by Thread Zero.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.452
The multiply has not written the result to the output array.

00:01:28.672 --> 00:01:29.888
That needs a store.

00:01:31.908 --> 00:01:33.892
The store is a separate instruction.

00:01:34.112 --> 00:01:42.261
Load and store units write two point zero from the thread’s result register into output index zero in GPU memory.

00:01:42.481 --> 00:01:44.700
The result is now in the output array.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.872
We have followed the whole value path: memory to register, arithmetic, then register to memory.

00:01:52.892 --> 00:01:56.284
Now inspect Thread One, whose input is two point zero.

00:01:56.504 --> 00:01:59.085
Its load supplies its own working register.

00:01:59.305 --> 00:02:01.609
Thread Zero’s values remain separate.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:06.971
We are changing which values we inspect, not choosing the GPU’s execution order.

00:02:07.191 --> 00:02:15.724
A GPU can execute instructions for groups of threads together; this picture isolates two threads to make their values easy to follow.

00:02:17.744 --> 00:02:23.760
Thread One’s multiply uses the same depicted floating point hardware, producing four point zero.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.881
Its store writes that result to output index one.

00:02:27.101 --> 00:02:31.112
Each thread has its own working values, while execution units are shared.

00:02:31.332 --> 00:02:36.495
The input and result columns identify values, not fixed physical registers.

00:02:36.715 --> 00:02:40.512
A register can be reused after a value is no longer needed.

00:02:40.732 --> 00:02:46.236
The boxes show hardware roles, not unit counts or exactly when instructions execute.

00:02:48.256 --> 00:02:49.557
Try one more case.

00:02:49.777 --> 00:02:53.489
A third thread loads three point zero and multiplies it by two.

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:55.587
Its store instruction has not run.

00:02:55.807 --> 00:03:00.308
Where is the result at this point: in a working register, or in the output array?

00:03:00.528 --> 00:03:02.213
Pause here and explain why.

00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:07.647
The result, six point zero, is in a working register.

00:03:07.867 --> 00:03:10.384
The store is what writes it to the output array.

00:03:10.604 --> 00:03:17.196
That is the distinction to take back to Chapter One: threads describe executions with separate working values.

00:03:17.416 --> 00:03:21.533
The S M provides shared hardware for loads, arithmetic and stores.

00:03:21.753 --> 00:03:26.745
This is a conceptual explanation, not a trace of the kernel PyTorch selected.
